; ==============================================================================
; OfficeHistory.rgr — what "one undo" means, in one place.
; ==============================================================================
;
; Three editors here keep an undo history and each wrote its own rules. The
; spreadsheet's are right; the document's were missing, and every one of the
; three things it was missing was a bug a user could hit:
;
;   * NO TRANSACTIONS. Pasting three lines pushed five ops, so getting back
;     took five undos — and did not get back at all: twenty presses later the
;     document still did not match what it had been.
;   * UNDO AND REDO AS TWO SWITCHES. `undo` handled five op kinds and `redo`
;     handled one, and redo pushed the op onto the undo stack anyway. So
;     redoing a multi-paragraph delete did nothing, and the NEXT undo ran that
;     op a second time and invented text that had never been in the document.
;   * AN UNNAMED KIND. `pasteChart` recorded `kind = 5`, which is the
;     multi-paragraph delete, because 5 was one more than the table paste's 4.
;     Undoing a chart paste therefore ran the delete's restore path and left
;     the chart where it was.
;
; None of those is a hard problem. They are what happens when the rules live
; in three places and only one of them is complete.
;
; WHAT IS SHARED — AND, AS OF GENERICS, THE OPS THEMSELVES.
;
; This file used to end its own explanation with an apology:
;
;     `SheetSetCell` is not `SlideMoveShape` is not `DocInsertText`, and a base
;     class over them would be a place to put nothing. Ranger has no generics
;     either, so this deliberately does not try to hold the ops themselves —
;     each editor keeps its own array.
;
; The first half of that is still true and the second half is not. A type
; parameter is not a base class: `OfficeHistory@(DocEditOp)` and
; `OfficeHistory@(SpreadsheetUndoOp)` are two separate concrete classes after
; the compiler expands them, neither of which knows anything about the other's
; operations. Nothing is asked of `Op` — no bound, no interface, no shared
; field — so a shape, a snapshot and a record all fit.
;
; So the array lives here now, and with it every rule about the array:
;
;   begin / end     what counts as one action, nested safely
;   record          stamping an op with its transaction, and killing redo
;   the cap         how many ops fall off the bottom — always whole
;                   transactions, so a surviving op can never be half of an
;                   action, and never mid-action
;   undoSpan        how many ops off the top are one undo
;
; And one discipline, which is not code but is the reason two of the three
; bugs above existed: UNDO AND REDO ARE ONE FUNCTION WITH A DIRECTION. Written
; as two switches over the same kind they drift, and the drift is silent until
; a user presses redo. Every pair below is that one function twice.
;
; TWO KINDS OF EDITOR, ONE SET OF RULES.
;
; The four editors do not agree on what an entry IS, and they do not have to:
;
;   * The document and the spreadsheet record OPERATIONS and undo by inverting
;     them. One press of undo is a whole transaction, so they walk `undoSpan`
;     ops with `peekUndo` / `moveUndoToRedo` and the mirror — one op at a
;     time, because an op that will not apply has to stay where it is.
;   * The book and the deck record SNAPSHOTS and undo by swapping states. One
;     entry is one press either way, so they use `popUndo` / `pushRedo` and
;     the mirror, and decide themselves which state crosses.
;
; Both get the cap, the transaction stamping and the redo invalidation from
; here, which is the whole point: those are the rules that were wrong in two
; editors out of three.
; ==============================================================================

; The two rules that are pure arithmetic over transaction ids, kept callable
; on their own. A static of a generic class can only be reached through an
; instantiated name (`OfficeHistory_DocEditOp.dropCount`), which is not a name
; anybody should have to write, so these live on a plain class.
class OfficeHistoryRules {

    ; How many ops to drop from the BOTTOM so the stack fits. Always whole
    ; transactions: dropping half of one leaves an undo that can only put a
    ; document into a state it was never in.
    sfn dropCount:int (txIds:[int] limit:int) {
        def n:int (array_length txIds)
        if (n <= limit) {
            return 0
        }
        def drop:int (n - limit)
        ; Walk forward past the rest of whichever transaction the cut lands in.
        if (drop < n) {
            def tx:int (itemAt txIds (drop - 1))
            if (tx > 0) {
                while (drop < n) {
                    if ((itemAt txIds drop) == tx) {
                        drop = (drop + 1)
                    } {
                        break
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return drop
    }

    ; How many ops off the TOP are one press of undo: the topmost op, plus
    ; every op below it in the same transaction. An op stamped 0 is alone.
    sfn spanAt:int (txIds:[int] top:int) {
        if (top < 0) {
            return 0
        }
        if (top >= (array_length txIds)) {
            return 0
        }
        def tx:int (itemAt txIds top)
        if (tx <= 0) {
            return 1
        }
        def count:int 1
        def i:int (top - 1)
        while (i >= 0) {
            if ((itemAt txIds i) == tx) {
                count = (count + 1)
                i = (i - 1)
            } {
                return count
            }
        }
        return count
    }
}

class OfficeHistory @params(Op) {
    ; How many ops to keep. Whole transactions are dropped, so the real length
    ; can sit under this rather than exactly on it.
    def limit:int 200
    ; Nesting depth of `begin` … `end`. An editor that composes actions out of
    ; other actions — a paste that is an insert and a split and another insert
    ; — nests, and only the outermost pair closes the transaction.
    def txDepth:int 0
    def txCounter:int 0
    def curTx:int 0

    ; The operations. Oldest first, so the top of the undo stack is the last
    ; element. `txIds` runs parallel to `ops`: one transaction id per op, which
    ; is what makes a cap able to drop whole actions.
    def ops:[Op]
    def txIds:[int]
    ; What undo has taken off. Also oldest first, so the next redo is its last
    ; element.
    def redoOps:[Op]
    def redoTxIds:[int]

    Constructor () {
    }

    fn reset:void () {
        txDepth = 0
        curTx = 0
        clear ops
        clear txIds
        clear redoOps
        clear redoTxIds
    }

    ; --- one action -----------------------------------------------------------

    fn begin:void () {
        if (txDepth == 0) {
            txCounter = (txCounter + 1)
            curTx = txCounter
        }
        txDepth = (txDepth + 1)
    }

    fn end:void () {
        if (txDepth > 0) {
            txDepth = (txDepth - 1)
        }
        if (txDepth == 0) {
            curTx = 0
        }
        if (txDepth == 0) {
            this.trim()
        }
    }

    fn inTransaction:boolean () {
        return (txDepth > 0)
    }

    ; The transaction id an op pushed right now belongs to. Zero means "on its
    ; own" — a single keystroke outside any `begin`.
    fn stamp:int () {
        return curTx
    }

    ; Trimming mid-transaction would drop the first half of an action and keep
    ; the second, so it waits for the action to finish.
    fn mayTrim:boolean () {
        return (txDepth == 0)
    }

    ; --- the array ------------------------------------------------------------

    ; The one way an op enters the history: stamped with the action it belongs
    ; to, and with the redo stack dropped, because a new edit makes every
    ; undone op unreachable — redoing one after it would apply it to a document
    ; it was never recorded against.
    fn record:void (op:Op) {
        push ops op
        push txIds curTx
        clear redoOps
        clear redoTxIds
        this.trim()
    }

    fn depth:int () {
        return (array_length ops)
    }

    fn redoDepth:int () {
        return (array_length redoOps)
    }

    fn canUndo:boolean () {
        return ((array_length ops) > 0)
    }

    fn canRedo:boolean () {
        return ((array_length redoOps) > 0)
    }

    fn opAt:Op (i:int) {
        def v:Op (itemAt ops i)
        return v
    }

    ; How many ops off the top are one press of undo.
    fn undoSpan:int () {
        def n:int (array_length ops)
        return (OfficeHistoryRules.spanAt(txIds (n - 1)))
    }

    ; Drop whole transactions off the bottom until the cap is met. Does
    ; nothing while an action is still being recorded.
    fn trim:void () {
        if (txDepth > 0) {
            return
        }
        def drop:int (OfficeHistoryRules.dropCount(txIds limit))
        def k:int 0
        while (k < drop) {
            remove_index ops 0
            remove_index txIds 0
            k = (k + 1)
        }
    }

    ; --- one press, for an editor that inverts operations ---------------------
    ;
    ; UNDO AND REDO ARE ONE FUNCTION WITH A DIRECTION. Written as two switches
    ; over the same op kind they drift, and the drift is silent until a user
    ; presses redo — which is exactly how redoing a multi-paragraph delete came
    ; to do nothing and then invent text on the NEXT undo.
    ;
    ; One op at a time rather than a whole action in one call, because an op
    ; that cannot be applied has to stay where it is: moving it anyway is how
    ; the two stacks come apart. The caller walks `undoSpan()` ops and stops
    ; early if one of them refuses.

    fn peekUndo:Op () {
        def n:int (array_length ops)
        def v:Op (itemAt ops (n - 1))
        return v
    }

    fn peekRedo:Op () {
        def n:int (array_length redoOps)
        def v:Op (itemAt redoOps (n - 1))
        return v
    }

    ; The op crosses WITH its transaction id, so the action it belonged to is
    ; still one press on the way back.
    fn moveUndoToRedo:void () {
        def n:int (array_length ops)
        def top:int (n - 1)
        push redoOps (itemAt ops top)
        push redoTxIds (itemAt txIds top)
        remove_index ops top
        remove_index txIds top
    }

    fn moveRedoToUndo:void () {
        def n:int (array_length redoOps)
        def top:int (n - 1)
        push ops (itemAt redoOps top)
        push txIds (itemAt redoTxIds top)
        remove_index redoOps top
        remove_index redoTxIds top
    }

    ; How many ops off the top of the REDO side are one press of redo. The
    ; ops crossed one at a time and kept their stamps, so this is the same
    ; question `undoSpan` asks, of the other stack.
    fn redoSpan:int () {
        def n:int (array_length redoTxIds)
        return (OfficeHistoryRules.spanAt(redoTxIds (n - 1)))
    }

    ; --- one press, for an editor that swaps whole states ---------------------
    ;
    ; A snapshot editor decides for itself which state crosses to the other
    ; side — the book keeps the live document outside the history, the deck
    ; keeps it as the top entry — so it takes one entry off and puts another
    ; one on, rather than moving one across. The cap, the stamping and the
    ; redo invalidation are still this file's, which is the part that was
    ; wrong in two editors out of three.

    fn popUndo:Op () {
        def n:int (array_length ops)
        def top:int (n - 1)
        def op:Op (itemAt ops top)
        remove_index ops top
        remove_index txIds top
        return op
    }

    fn popRedo:Op () {
        def n:int (array_length redoOps)
        def top:int (n - 1)
        def op:Op (itemAt redoOps top)
        remove_index redoOps top
        remove_index redoTxIds top
        return op
    }

    ; Put a state on the redo side without touching the undo side. Never
    ; trims: the redo stack is bounded by the undo stack it came from.
    fn pushRedo:void (op:Op) {
        push redoOps op
        push redoTxIds curTx
    }

    ; Put a state back on the undo side, on a redo. Not `record`: a redo is not
    ; a new edit, so it must not drop the redo stack it is walking.
    fn pushUndo:void (op:Op) {
        push ops op
        push txIds curTx
        this.trim()
    }

    ; Replace the newest entry instead of adding one: a burst of typing is one
    ; step, not one step per keystroke. The redo side is left alone — this is
    ; not a new entry, it is the same one, changed.
    fn replaceTop:void (op:Op) {
        def n:int (array_length ops)
        set_at ops (n - 1) op
    }

    fn clearRedo:void () {
        clear redoOps
        clear redoTxIds
    }
}
